A||||6||poupas||confusion with electronics||||1||
Z||000000||poupas||10-07-2001||03:10 PM||||Hey everyone. I am having serious(or maybe not so serious) problem. Yesterday I shut off my car-'86 6cylinder-and I heard a humming sound, with the car off. It sounded like the heater fan was running. It wasnt but had a similar sound, and just as loud. Needless to say something was drawing power that shouldnt have been. So, I put a charger on the battery overnight and when I came back the next morning there was no power at all, no gauge lights, nothing. So I disconnected the battery and put it on the charger. Just now I went back out and the battery read 9V on the multimeter, when I hooked it up to the car I still had absolutely no power and the multimeter read 0.26V. Any diagnosis????||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-07-2001||03:25 PM||||Purely a guess, but I wonder if your fuel pump continues to run after shutdown?  Apparently, it has been known to happen (luckily, not to me, yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>If you think that's the case, the oil pressure sending unit (near the battery) may be the culprit.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000002||Mark||10-07-2001||07:15 PM||||Sounds like you may have several problems.  First, your disconnected battery didn't charge overnight.  Sounds like you a) didn't make good connections from the charger to the battery, b) have a bad charger or battery, or d) damaged a cable/connection when you disconnected then reconnected the battery.  Since you see a big voltage drop, I suspect you may have a problem with your battery cables/connections.<P>As for the second problem.  I could be the fuel pump.  Since you can actually hear it run, the diagnosis should be pretty easy.  Isolate/identify the problem circuit or component by pulling fuses (one at a time) until the "noise" stops.   <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 10-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||10-07-2001||08:19 PM||||I guess you already checked this, but when my radiator fan relay stuck, it would run till the battery was dead after shutoff. You couldn't have missed it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||poupas||10-09-2001||11:44 PM||||Thanks for the help for those who responded, but there are still some problems. I cant start pulling out fuses because when I now connect the battery the voltage drops to nothing so there is no power to hear this component run. The battery cables are fine. The battery is only four months old, and the only problem I have had with it has occured after this incident. I did not charge the battery overnight, just a few hours, on a trikle charger. It is not the fan. <BR>   Could I have blew this component by letting it run overnight and now it is causing a short. If I had a major short would it cause the voltage to drop so dramatically when the battery was connected.<BR>Thanks for any insight, it is greatly appreciated.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||10-10-2001||12:31 AM||||You could just have a bad battery.  Check the water in it too.  Trickle chargers take a long time to charge.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cowans||10-10-2001||02:02 PM||||"Poupas", First, you want to disconnect the + cable before charging. Sometimes, if connected, the altenator will ni-gate the charge you are trying to give, especially, a low-ampere(trikle) charge. You say your battery is new.... Newer batteries, when discharged, charge differently than the older types, almost comparable to a ni-cad battery (with a memory).If you heavy (mucho amps)charge the new battery, it will only 1/2 charge it, much like a ni-cad. The best way is with an Automatic charger overnight, cables off, (will stop & start on its own). troubles still...I'm in your area,  ...-781-9653 during the day.<BR>Sandy<BR>||||reg||1
